<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Madame de Pompadour</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1721">1721</a>–<a href="/years/1764">1764</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Jeanne Antoinette Poisson, Marquise de Pompadour</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jeanne Antoinette Poisson, Marquise de Pompadour, was a member of the French court who was the official chief mistress of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xv">Louis XV</a> from 1745 to 1751 and remained influential as court favourite until her death. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1721, she moved in the salons of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>, and the king gave her the marquisate of Pompadour in 1745; she was made a duchess in 1752 and lady-in-waiting to the queen in 1756. She shaped appointments and domestic and foreign policy, patronised the philosophes, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a>, and bought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a> factory at Sèvres in 1759. She died of tuberculosis in 1764 at the age of 42.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Noblewomen and rich bourgeois women presided over the salons of Paris, meeting places of the Enlightenment, and a few won fame in their own right: Émilie du Châtelet translated Newton’s Principia into French, and Jeanne-Antoinette Poisson, a commoner, became the <b>Marquise de Pompadour</b> and Louis XV’s adviser.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ancien-regime-france">Social Hierarchy of Ancien Régime France</a></li></ul></section>
